          {
            title: 'Joffre',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Joffre is a traditional QuÃ©bÃ©cois trick-taking game played in the Bellechasse region of Canada. A relative of the Saskatchewan game 'Kaiser', it features a 32-card deck and two highly significant cards: the 'Joffre' (5 of Hearts, scoring +5) and the 'Kaiser' (3 of Spades, scoring -3). It is a game of high variance where one bad trick can lose the round.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Brought back to Quebec by soldier OnÃ©sime LalibertÃ© after World War I. Named after the French General Joseph Joffre, it reflects the wartime era of its popularization.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in two partnerships.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32 cards. Traditionally: 7-Ace in each suit, BUT the 7 of Hearts is replaced by the 5 of Hearts, and the 7 of Spades is replaced by the 3 of Spades.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '32 cards. Traditionally: 7-Ace in each suit, BUT the 7 of Hearts is replaced by the 5 of Hearts, and the 7 of Spades is replaced by the 3 of Spades.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deal 8 cards to each player.',
              },
            ],
